  "response": "Disputes of this kind are adjudicated first by the eye and only afterwards by the instrument. Connoisseurship compares the brushwork of a disputed picture with securely attributed works, treating handling, touch and the articulation of detail as the signature of a particular hand. Seventeenth-century workshops, by contrast, routinely produced replicas from the master's models, so that an entirely persuasive touch may belong to a well-drilled assistant. Whereas such replication proceeds from a design already fixed before the brush is lifted, pentimenti in the paint layers indicate that the composition was invented on the support.\n\nLaboratory examination of the paint film is the next stage of the inquiry, and it turns on the dating of materials. X-ray fluorescence spectroscopy identifies the chemical elements present in a painting's pigments, and its elemental readings confirm that titanium white was a standard pigment in seventeenth-century Flemish painting. That Prussian blue was first synthesized only in the early eighteenth century shows that the presence of an anachronistic pigment excludes an attribution to an earlier master.\n\nThe support itself is interrogated in the same spirit, since a panel can be dated independently of what is painted on it. Dendrochronology establishes the earliest possible date for an oak panel from its growth-ring pattern. The exact year in which a panel painting was executed is fixed by dendrochronological measurement. Applied to the picture under dispute, such ring analysis indicates that its wood dates from before the master's death. Either that early dating for the timber holds, or the wood of the disputed panel dates from after the master's death: one of the two must be the case, and they cannot both be.\n\nThe latter dating indicates that the tribunal held that the painting was not executed by the master himself. The disputed painting is an autograph work by the master; that is, the master painted the disputed picture with his own hand. A panel laid in and finished by the master's own hand cannot be squared with the finding recorded by the tribunal, and it is this friction between the physical record and the documentary one that the attribution must finally resolve. What settles the question, in practice, is not any single observation but the point at which the material evidence, the technical evidence and the archival record can be made to tell one consistent story about who stood at the easel.",
